<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/tornado-aerial.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/tornado-aerial-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/tornado-aerial.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="576" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/service-cities/">Service cities</a></li><li>Xenia</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Southwest Ohio · Greene County</p><h1>Xenia, Ohio Insurance Appraiser</h1><p class="lede">Few cities are as closely tied to a single storm as Xenia. As an insurance appraiser in Xenia, Ohio, we help Greene County homeowners with the claims that tornadoes, wind and hail still bring.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Greene County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Southwest Ohio</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="snap"><div><small>County</small><b>Greene County</b></div><div><small>Region</small><b>Southwest Ohio</b></div><div class="wide"><small>Housing mix</small><span>Much of Xenia was rebuilt after 1974, so ranch and split-level homes from the 1970s sit alongside surviving older brick houses downtown and newer subdivisions nearby.</span></div><div class="wide"><small>Storm history</small><span>The April 3, 1974 Xenia tornado, an F5 in the Super Outbreak, devastated the city, and an EF3 hit Beavercreek during the Memorial Day 2019 outbreak.</span></div><div class="wide"><small>Local claim risks</small><ul class="chips"><li>Tornadoes</li><li>Hail</li><li>Straight-line wind</li><li>Fallen trees</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul></div></div>
<h2>The storm that defined Xenia</h2>
<p>On April 3, 1974, an F5 tornado struck Xenia as part of the Super Outbreak that swept much of the eastern United States. It destroyed a large share of the city’s homes, schools and businesses. The rebuilding that followed shaped much of the town as it stands today. In Greene County, tornado risk did not end in 1974: during the Memorial Day 2019 outbreak, an EF3 hit Beavercreek in the western part of the county.</p>
<h2>A rebuilt city and its neighbors</h2>
<p>Because so much of Xenia was rebuilt after 1974, many neighborhoods are filled with ranch and split-level homes from the 1970s, now roughly half a century old. Older brick buildings survive downtown, and newer subdivisions have grown on the edges of town. Beavercreek and Bellbrook carry larger suburban homes, Fairborn has a mix of post-war and newer housing, and Yellow Springs, Cedarville and Wilberforce add older village homes and college-area rentals.</p>
<h2>Greene County claims that need a second look</h2>
<ul>
<li>Roofs on 1970s homes depreciated heavily, with the replacement-cost holdback never pursued.</li>
<li>Hail damage to aluminum siding, gutters and trim left off a roof-only estimate.</li>
<li>Wind claims denied as wear when shingle seal strips failed across whole slopes.</li>
<li>Tree damage where removal costs exceed a small debris-removal sublimit.</li>
<li>Tornado losses where code upgrades and additional living expense are underestimated.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use the <a href="/appraisal-workbench/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV/ACV holdback calculator</a> to see how much depreciation is being held back, and how much you may be able to recover once repairs are complete.</p>
<p>Homes from the 1970s present their own estimating questions. Aluminum siding and older trim profiles can be hard to match, roofs may have a second layer, and windows and gutters are often original. An insurance appraiser in Xenia, Ohio looks at whether a repair can truly match what is there, or whether a whole elevation needs replacing for a uniform result.</p>
<h2>How we help in Xenia</h2>
<p>We inspect the property, document every damaged surface and write an estimate priced for the Miami Valley market. We then compare it with the insurer’s numbers and explain the difference clearly. If your policy has an appraisal clause, we can act as your appraiser and pursue an award that reflects the real cost of repair. Our <a href="/appraisal-practice/tornado-windstorm-appraisal/">tornado and windstorm appraisal</a> service covers how we approach major wind events in detail.</p>
<h3>Preparing before the next storm</h3>
<p>Xenia’s history is a good reason to review your coverage before spring. Check your dwelling limit against current building costs, look at your ordinance-or-law coverage and additional living expense, and keep an up-to-date home inventory with photos.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Know your limits</b>Rebuilding costs rise over time. A dwelling limit that was right ten years ago may be too low today. Check your policy each year.</div>
<h2>Neighborhoods and nearby communities</h2><p>We inspect in and around Xenia, including Downtown Xenia, Beavercreek, Fairborn, Yellow Springs, Cedarville, Wilberforce, Bellbrook, Jamestown, and anywhere else in Greene County and the surrounding counties.</p>
